    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d2">

                <pb facs="0103" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg1"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President’s Special Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to Prime Minister Churchill
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d2-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-02-24">February 24, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Apropos of your request to me regarding 
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tube Alloys</hi>, I have made some
                    inquiry here.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d2-f2"/> It would be very helpful if Anderson
                    could send me by pouch a full memorandum of what he considers to be the basis of
                    the present misunderstanding, particularly the copies of the original memoranda
                    or any references or conversations which form the bases <pb facs="0104" n="2"
                        xml:id="pg2"/> of the misunderstanding. In a casual inquiry here I find that
                    our people feel that there has been no breach of agreement, but I want to go
                    into it thoroughly and a memorandum from Anderson would help.</p>
                <p>I do hope you are feeling better.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d2-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d2-f1-content">
                <p>Manner of transmission not indicated, but presumably by military channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d2-f2" n="2" xml:id="d2-f2-content">
                <p>The Hopkins Papers include a memorandum of February 26, 1943, from Bush to
                    Hopkins, and an enclosed 22-page file of documents bearing on the interchange of
                    scientific information with the British, particularly the initial
                    American–British arrangements of September and October, 1940. According to the
                    Bush memorandum, the file was prepared following a conversation between Hopkins
                    and Bush.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
